pply mission (in August 2021 and again later). These included samples of the detergent, stain-treated fabrics, and a small-scale experiment to test how the cleaning agents and stains behave in microgravity, particularly how fluids interact with fabrics in space. The results helped refine the detergent performance and machine design. - **Ground-based tests.** A prototype washing machine has been built and tested on Earth inside mock-ups of ISS modules and even on zero-gravity parabolic flights (the "vomit comet") to understand water removal and drying cycles. - **Drying and moisture recapture.** A big challenge is not just washing, but *drying* clothes without a conventional dryer. The concept uses a combination of air circulation, absorbent materials, and heat to capture moisture so it can be condensed and fed back into the water loop. Trials of this drying subsystem are ongoing. ### Why Isn't a Laundry Machine on the ISS Yet? The traditional method (wear-and-discard) still works fine for low-earth orbit because resupply ships visit frequently. But for a 2–3 year round-trip Mars mission, carrying a complete new set of clothes for each crew member per change isn't practical—the mass and volume would be prohibitive. That’s the driving force behind the Tide partnership. The current timeline suggests that a flight-ready laundry system could be installed on the ISS later this decade (possibly around 2026–2028) for long-term testing, with the real payoff coming for Artemis and Mars-class missions. Until then, astronauts continue with the wipes and disposal method I first described. So your memory is spot-on: NASA and Tide are actively developing space laundry, and one day astronauts *will* do their own washing in orbit. For now, they’re still waiting for that first spin cycle in space.
